<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/foundational/form-follows-forces">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the beginner lesson of Architecture's technical strand — how buildings
    actually stand up. You need no prior knowledge: no physics course, no drawing
    skills, no construction vocabulary. The first experiment uses a sheet of paper, and
    every term is defined the moment it appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the difference between a beam and an arch — bending versus compression — and why stone prefers the arch;</li>
    <li>describe the hanging-chain trick (the catenary) and Hooke's inversion of it;</li>
    <li>name the established systems — masonry, arch, vault, dome, Gothic, post-and-beam, steel frame, reinforced concrete — and say how each carries load;</li>
    <li>assess any technology against three checks: design fit, economics, performance over life;</li>
    <li>trace a load path from roof to ground in a house and in a tower.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Take your time. Each paragraph carries one idea, and the whole lesson turns on a
    single distinction — pulling versus pushing — that you will meet in the first
    experiment.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a sheet of paper</h2>
  <p>
    Lay a sheet of paper across two stacks of books, a hand-width apart, and rest a
    coin in the middle. The paper sags and the coin slides off. Now fold the same sheet
    into a pleated fan, or roll it into a tube, and try again: the coin sits easily.
    Nothing about the material changed. The <em>shape</em> changed — and with it, the
    strength. That is the deepest fact in building: strength is not only what something
    is made of, but how its shape lets forces flow through it. Architects compress the
    whole lesson into a phrase: <em>form follows forces</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two structural characters run the entire subject. A <strong>beam</strong> carries
    load by <em>bending</em>: one face of it stretches (that is <strong>tension</strong>,
    pulling), the other face squeezes (that is <strong>compression</strong>, pushing),
    and the middle does expensive work. An <strong>arch</strong> carries the same load
    by <em>pure compression</em>: every stone simply pushes on its neighbour, and
    nothing anywhere is pulled. The distinction matters because materials have
    preferences. Stone is strong in compression and weak in tension — which is why the
    Romans, who had no steel, built arches that still stand two thousand years later.
    The Pont du Gard aqueduct carries water across a river on three tiers of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the same material, reshaped, carries vastly more — and
    every structural choice is a choice between bending (beams) and pushing (arches).
    Now the shape that carries by pushing alone, discovered by hanging a chain.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the hanging chain and the parabola — nearly identical shapes, subtly different physics" widget="plotter" config={archCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag the curvature slider and watch both curves
    sharpen and flatten together while keeping their subtle difference — the catenary
    climbs slightly steeper toward the ends, where the parabola stays shallower. For
    shallow arches the two shapes nearly coincide, which is why the difference is so easy
    to miss and so important to know. The chain carries its own weight along the curve
    (catenary); a bridge deck spreads weight evenly along the span (parabola); Hooke's
    inversion turns either shape into a compression-only arch.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hang a chain from its two ends and it droops into a curve called a
    <strong>catenary</strong> (from Latin <em>catena</em>, chain). In that curve, the
    chain carries its own weight in pure tension — pure pulling. Robert Hooke described
    the trick in 1675, hiding the wording as an anagram to keep the credit: "as hangs
    the flexible line, so but inverted will stand the rigid arch." Flip the curve
    upside down and the pure pulling becomes pure pushing: an arch shaped like an
    inverted chain carries load with zero bending. The parabola plotted alongside is
    its cousin — correct when weight is spread evenly <em>along the span</em> (a bridge
    deck) rather than along the curve itself. For shallow arches the two shapes nearly
    coincide, which is why the difference confuses even engineers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trick still earns its keep. Antoni Gaudí hung weighted chains upside down to
    design the Colònia Güell church, letting gravity itself compute the
    compression-only geometry — a method he used across his Barcelona works, including
    the Sagrada Família. The Gateway Arch in St Louis (1965) is the same idea at
    monument scale: a flattened, weighted catenary in stainless steel, standing because
    every section pushes on the next.
  </p>

  <h2>Established systems: the load-bearing canon</h2>
  <p>
    The established technologies form a short canon. Each is an answer to one question:
    how does load reach the ground?
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Load-bearing masonry</strong> — walls that <em>are</em> the structure —
    governed building for five millennia. Its constraint: openings weaken walls, and
    height is limited by the mortar's crushing strength. Hence the thick,
    small-windowed architecture of fortresses and old churches.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The arch, vault and dome</strong> escape the span limit of a plain stone
    lintel. A barrel vault is a continuous arch; a dome is an arch rotated on its axis.
    The Pantheon's 43-metre unreinforced concrete dome (about 126 CE) remains the
    world's largest — and its builders graded the concrete from heavy basalt at the
    base to featherweight pumice at the top, an early exercise in exactly the material
    optimisation this subject teaches.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Gothic structural skeletons</strong> (12th–13th centuries) refined the arch
    into pointed ribs and <strong>flying buttresses</strong> — external struts that
    catch the sideways thrust of the vaults and deliver it to the ground. Stone walls
    turned from structure into curtain, which is why cathedrals could dissolve into
    glass.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Post-and-beam frames</strong> are the other great family — timber framing,
    classical colonnades, and their modern descendants — carrying load through bending
    members rather than compression arcs. The 1880s broke the height barrier with two
    paired inventions: the <strong>safety elevator</strong> (Otis, 1850s) and the
    <strong>steel skeleton frame</strong> (Chicago's Home Insurance Building, 1885).
    Steel is strong in tension, so beams could span far and frames could stack floor
    upon floor — and the skyscraper, its outer walls hung off the structure like a coat
    on a hook, became possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Reinforced concrete</strong> (Hennebique's system, 1890s) merged both
    material virtues in one composite. Concrete carries compression; steel reinforcing
    bars — <strong>rebar</strong> — carry tension. The two are partners because they
    expand at nearly the same rate with heat, and the alkaline concrete protects the
    steel from rust.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nearly every building you have ever entered is some assembly of these. Slabs
    transfer to beams, beams to columns or walls, walls to foundations, foundations to
    ground. That chain is the <strong>load path</strong> — the single idea every
    structural drawing is secretly about.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the arch does not "hold" by mortar gluing stones together —
  it holds by geometry. Cut the mortar away and a well-built arch can still stand, each wedge-shaped
  voussoir locked by compression; what it cannot survive is having its shape altered, because the
  shape <em>is</em> the load path. Architects say form follows forces because, in compression
  structures, the sentence is literal.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the canon answers one question five ways — masonry pushes
    through walls, arches and domes push along curves, Gothic routes the push outside,
    frames bend, and reinforced concrete partners pushing with pulling. Now: how an
    architect chooses among them.
  </p>

  <h2>Assessing technologies: design, economics, performance</h2>
  <p>
    Knowing the systems is half the job. The other half is <em>choosing</em> among
    them, and honest assessment runs three checks.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first is <strong>design fit</strong>: does the technology serve the building's
    intent? Span and grid set the first test — a column-free auditorium demands long
    spans (steel trusses or concrete shells), while a laboratory tolerates tight grids.
    Services integration, future flexibility and even acoustics follow. The 20th-century
    modernists built the aesthetic case for this: Sullivan's dictum that "form ever
    follows function" and Mies van der Rohe's "less is more" were, structurally,
    arguments that the honest expression of the load path <em>is</em> the architecture —
    which is why exposed frames and visible services became a style rather than an
    accident.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second check is <strong>economics</strong>: cost is structure plus time plus
    risk. Material cost is only the start. Crane hours matter: prefabrication shifts
    labour from the site, where weather and sequencing bleed money, to the factory,
    where it is fast and repeatable. Foundation cost matters: steel's lightness can
    save more below ground than it spends above. And speed matters: every month of
    construction is interest and rent foregone.
  </p>
  <p>
    The third check is <strong>performance over life</strong>: maintenance, durability,
    fire, heat, and carbon. Steel loses strength dramatically near 500–600 °C and must
    be fire-protected; concrete is naturally fire-resistant. Masonry's mass smooths
    temperature swings; glass towers need engineering to avoid becoming greenhouses.
    And carbon is now a first-order criterion: concrete's cement alone is responsible
    for roughly 7–8 percent of global CO<sub>2</sub> emissions, so embodied carbon has
    moved from sustainability footnote to design driver.
  </p>
  <p>
    The professional skill is that the three checks trade against each other. The
    cheapest frame is rarely the fastest; the fastest is rarely the most flexible. The
    assessment memo — not the shape model — is where architecture decides what it will
    be.
  </p>

  <h2>Emerging systems: what is changing now</h2>
  <p>
    The current generation of technologies is answering the carbon and speed checks —
    and each one is easier to understand once you know the canon.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mass timber</strong> means panels and beams built up from small boards —
    cross-laminated timber (CLT) glues layers crosswise, which cancels wood's
    direction-dependent weaknesses. It now builds mid- and high-rise structures: the
    85.4-metre Mjøstårnet in Norway (2019) was the world's tallest timber building at
    completion. The embodied carbon is a fraction of concrete's, the old post-and-beam
    logic returns with factory precision, and fire behaves differently than you might
    expect — heavy timber chars slowly at its surface instead of conducting heat the
    way a steel frame does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Prefabrication and modular construction</strong> push the factory logic to
    whole room-volumes, stacked like bricks. <strong>3D-printed construction</strong>
    extrudes mortar layer by layer, eliminating <em>formwork</em> — the moulds that
    shape wet concrete, and the hidden cost inside every curved concrete shape.
  </p>
  <p>
    Formwork is why Félix Candela's thin shells were heroic. His 1940s–50s hyperbolic
    paraboloid vaults achieved long spans in concrete centimetres thick by pure
    geometry — form resisting load the way the catenary does — but the timber moulds
    cost more than the concrete. Digital formwork removal (printing) and computational
    form-finding (the hanging-chain model, now run as finite-element software) are
    making those compression-efficient shapes affordable for the first time since the
    Romans. Shell structures and gridshells are returning, 3D-printed bridges included.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Adaptive and low-carbon assemblies</strong> round the list: double-skin
    facades that ventilate themselves, phase-change materials that store heat, and the
    quiet revolution of <em>reusing</em> existing structure (retrofit-first design) —
    because the lowest-carbon building is the one already standing.
  </p>

<p><em>Common misconception:</em> "in every building, the walls carry the load." That is true of the load-bearing tradition — and false for the framed one. In a steel or concrete frame, the columns and beams carry every load down to the ground while the walls hang off the frame as weather skin, which is why a glass tower can have no structural walls at all. Knowing which system you are standing in is the first question of any structural reading.</p>

  <h2>Reading a building's load path</h2>
  <p>
    The load path is the single idea every structural drawing is secretly about — and
    you can read it in any building you enter. Take an ordinary house. The
    <strong>roof</strong> is a pair of rafters leaning on each other — a primitive arch,
    its spreading thrust caught by a <strong>ceiling tie</strong> that works in tension,
    the member steel made easy. The roof's weight lands on <strong>walls</strong>, which
    are load-bearing masonry or timber studs sheathed so they resist racking. The walls
    land on <strong>foundation footings</strong>, whose job is to spread the same weight
    over enough soil area that the ground beneath does not fail. Soil is weak in
    everything but compression — it is the ultimate bottom of every path.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now a modern office tower. Glass panels carry only themselves — a
    <em>curtain wall</em>, hung from the frame like a sail. Floor <strong>slabs</strong>
    span to <strong>beams</strong>, beams to <strong>columns</strong> arranged on a
    regular grid, columns into a concrete core or pile caps, and down. The difference
    between the two buildings is not sophistication but <em>concentration</em>: the
    house spreads load through area (walls), the tower through lines (frames).
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the practical meaning of "assessing technologies": for the use you intend,
    which system carries the load with the least material, the least disruption, the
    least carbon — and the honest designer can trace every kilogram from its point of
    application to the earth. Every renovation begins by asking which members are on
    the path (untouchable) and which are mere infill (removable).
  </p>

<p><em>Common misconception:</em> "newer structural systems are automatically better than old ones." A stone arch and a steel truss obey the same statics; each succeeds or fails by how well it matches the load path, the site and the budget. Steel spans further and concrete moulds freely, but for a two-storey village house a load-bearing wall may still be the honest, cheapest, most repairable answer. Technology adds options; it does not retire physics.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Stone is strong in compression and weak in tension. Why did the Romans build arches rather than beams?</li>
    <li>A cathedral wall has visibly external struts catching the vault's sideways push. What are these called, and what do they do?</li>
    <li>In a glass office tower, do the glass walls carry the building's weight? Where does the weight go?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) A beam works by bending, which pulls one face apart
    (tension) — stone fails there; an arch carries in pure compression, so stone's
    strength is used and its weakness avoided. (2) Flying buttresses — they catch the
    sideways thrust of the vaults and deliver it to the ground, freeing the walls to
    become glass. (3) No — the glass is curtain wall, carrying only itself; the loads
    travel through slabs, beams and columns down the frame to the ground.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Strength lives in shape as well as material — the same sheet of paper, reshaped, carries vastly more.</li>
    <li>Beams carry load by bending (tension on one face, compression on the other); arches carry by pure compression.</li>
    <li>Stone is strong in compression and weak in tension, which is why the Romans built arches that still stand.</li>
    <li>A hanging chain falls into a catenary — pure tension; inverted, it becomes a compression-only arch (Hooke, 1675).</li>
    <li>The established canon answers one question five ways: masonry walls, arch-vault-dome, Gothic skeletons, post-and-beam frames, reinforced concrete.</li>
    <li>Every technology is assessed on three checks — design fit, economics, performance over life — and the checks trade against each other.</li>
    <li>The load path is the single idea every structural drawing is secretly about; an honest design can trace every kilogram to the ground.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Later lessons go deeper into each family — how frames are sized, how shells and other
    surface-active structures eliminate bending entirely, and how assessment is becoming
    quantitative with whole-building life-cycle analysis. The deeper section below threads
    the canon to the frontier, from tensegrity to decarbonisation.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three threads tie the canon to the frontier. The first is the
    <strong>hierarchy of structural systems</strong>: load-bearing walls, frames, and
    surface-active structures (shells, membranes, tensegrity). It is a hierarchy of how
    load reaches the ground — through area (walls), through lines (beams and columns),
    or through curved surfaces carrying load in their skin. Surface-active systems are
    the most material-efficient, because they eliminate bending — the expensive state —
    entirely. That is the deep reason the catenary is architecture's most honest shape,
    and why Buckminster Fuller spent a career on geodesics and tensegrity: structures
    held stable by pure tension in cables and pure compression in floating struts.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second thread is <strong>assembly knowledge</strong>, the craft layer. Between
    system and building sits the joint: how CLT panels connect, how a curtain wall
    thermally breaks from its slab, how the rebar laps at a column junction. Most
    real-world building failures — water ingress, thermal bridging, progressive
    collapse — are assembly failures, not system failures, which is why technical
    knowledge lives at the detail scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    The third is that <strong>assessment is finally becoming quantitative</strong>.
    Whole-building LCA (life-cycle assessment) software now prices the embodied carbon
    of a frame decision in tonnes of CO<sub>2</sub>-equivalent, and performance-based
    fire and seismic design lets engineers demonstrate safety by simulation rather than
    prescriptive code. Read the whole history once more and the through-line holds:
    Rome compressed, Gothic routed, Chicago framed, Hennebique partnered, Candela
    curved, and the present is decarbonising — each era a new agreement between form
    and force, and each agreement visible in the shape of the buildings it left
    standing.
  </p>
